From df7c68b7a7bb0e49524bb093ae9c08dbc33b6cab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 11:17:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] A site says when it is open, and the schedule has to
 agree
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The ask was "let me disable Sunday as a day classes can be scheduled on". What
Poolse did not have is the thing that makes that answerable once rather than per
feature: a place where a site's standing operating rules live. Turmas want it
now; reposições and maintenance windows will want the same answer, and asking it
three different ways is how they end up disagreeing.

`facility_hours` holds seven rows per site — ISO weekday, matching
class_schedule — seeded by a trigger so every reader is a plain SELECT rather
than a SELECT plus a defaulting rule that one of three readers will forget. The
default is open, all day: an invented 08:00–22:00 would silently invalidate data
that already exists, including this repo's own 23:30 Azores lane-hire test.

**Closing a day blocks the next class and leaves the ones already there alone.**
That is the decision, and a trigger on class_schedule is exactly its shape — it
fires when somebody schedules, never when somebody edits the hours. Refusing the
edit would deadlock the operator: they cannot close the day until the classes
move, and the reason the classes are moving is that the day is closing. So the
grid shows the count of turmas already on a day as it is switched off, before
the change rather than after. generate_sessions is deliberately untouched: made
to skip a newly-closed day, it would delete the sessions this decision keeps.

Hours bound when a class starts, not when it ends. The last lesson of the night
ends when it ends, and a weekly pattern cannot express a class crossing midnight
against a closing time anyway.

Per facility, not per pool: two pools in one building share a set of doors. If
one ever genuinely differs, this table gains a nullable pool_id and falls back —
cheaper to add than to collapse. It is a block on the site's own page rather
than a sidebar item, for the reason Reposições sits under Turmas: a settings
area invented for one feature is an area everything else drifts into. Closures
link out to the calendar, which already owns that table.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PaASymUKAx5c46ceXf6GY2
