From 94a91b0e1f88fb738d4ba30ea6c9ceb59fd4f7ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 11:28:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] A pool's water and a pool's cupboard, as two blocks of
 their own
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Neither belongs on the details form, and neither belongs on the create form:
somebody adding a pool is describing a tank, while its readings and its kit are
what accumulates afterwards. Asking for them at creation asks for answers at the
one moment nobody has them.

**Water quality is a placeholder, deliberately shaped like the real thing.** pH,
temperature and the analysis report are phase 4 — they are time-series, which is
what TimescaleDB is in the stack for, and CLAUDE.md is explicit that pH, °C and
ppm do not share a type. Building the table now, to hold nothing, would be
guessing at all of that a month early. So the panel gets the treatment the photo
controls get: present, laid out and visibly inert. An operator who finds no
mention of pH concludes Poolse does not do water quality; one who finds an empty
panel knows it is coming. The empty state is the layout the real one will use,
and the second disabled control is the import-a-report-and-read-the-values path.

**Inventory is real.** A free-text name and a count, one row per kind of item.
The obvious alternative — a dropdown of approved equipment types — fails on the
first club that keeps arcos, and then those arcos go in a notes field. It is
also deliberately not a stock ledger: no movements, no minimum levels. A ledger
nobody posts to drifts from the shelf within a month and is then wrong with more
precision than a count is.

The unique index is what stops "Flutuadores" and "flutuádores" being two piles
of the same floats, which is not a loud failure but a count that quietly stops
meaning anything — and it is partial, or archiving an item would make its name
permanently unusable. Both are asserted in packages/db/test/pool-materials.sql.

The Excel import the owner asked for is a disabled button. When it lands it goes
on the staged pipeline the backoffice already uses — upload, parse, map,
validate, commit — because real inventory spreadsheets have headers that match
nobody else's, and that is what the mapping step is for.

